<section class="tale-hero" id="top"><div class="tale-hero-copy"><nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/index.html">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/stories.html">Stories</a><span>/</span><span>Jada Bharata</span></nav><p class="eyebrow">SRIMAD BHAGAVATA PURANA / FIFTH SKANDHA</p><h1>A king, a deer,<br><em>and the wisdom of the Self.</em></h1><p class="tale-deck">He left a throne to find freedom, yet became bound by concern for a single fawn. Through three lives and a meeting with King Rahugana, Bharata learns how subtle attachment can be.</p><div class="tale-meta"><span>Bhagavata Purana · 5.7–14</span><span>About {{ storyCatalog.jada.minutes }} min read</span><a href="/sources.html#jada-bharata">View sources</a></div><a class="button" href="#renunciation">Begin the story</a></div><figure class="tale-hero-art"><img src="/assets/images/jada-bharata-deer.png" width="1400" height="788" fetchpriority="high" alt="King Bharata after renunciation tenderly cares for a young deer by a sunlit forest stream in cinematic artwork"><figcaption>BHARATA AND THE YOUNG DEER</figcaption></figure></section>
<div class="tale-introduction"><p>From the <strong>Srimad Bhagavata Purana, Fifth Skandha, chapters 7–14</strong>. The account follows Bharata through three births and his teaching to King Rahugana. This is original English prose, not a verbatim translation.</p></div>
<section class="story-characters" aria-labelledby="characters-title"><p class="eyebrow">MEET THE CHARACTERS</p><h2 id="characters-title">Characters in this story</h2><dl><div><dt>King Bharata</dt><dd>A ruler in Rishabha's lineage who renounces his kingdom for spiritual practice.</dd></div><div><dt>The deer</dt><dd>A helpless fawn whose rescue becomes an unexpected test of attachment.</dd></div><div><dt>Jada Bharata</dt><dd>Bharata in a later human birth, inwardly discerning but thought to be uncomprehending.</dd></div><div><dt>King Rahugana</dt><dd>A ruler whose anger with a palanquin bearer becomes a searching conversation.</dd></div><div><dt>The robbers</dt><dd>Men who attempt a cruel sacrifice and encounter the wrath of the Goddess.</dd></div></dl></section>
